IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `lpula-practice`

-- Legal Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2022 (Western Australia), with the Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) Part 4.3
-- Division 4 -- the fee arrangements a WA lawyer may make: whether Part 4.3 governs the matter (Uniform Law Sch 4 cl 18),
-- conditional costs agreements and uplift fees (Uniform Law ss 180-182), the ban on contingency fees and the State's costs
-- determinations as "fixed costs legislative provisions" (Uniform Law s 183, Application Act s 136), what follows from a breach
-- (Uniform Law ss 184, 185), and the State's own costs law: what a legal costs determination may cover and when it governs
-- (Application Act ss 3, 132-134, 140, 141), disclosure (s 143), and review of and recovery after a costs assessment
-- (ss 149, 151).
--
-- SEAT: the lawyer (Daniel, d038: "Acts for a client in an arbitration under a conditional costs agreement with a 25% uplift").
-- His questions: "May I act on a conditional costs agreement here?", "Is a 25% uplift allowed?", "Is this a contingency fee?",
-- "What happens if the agreement is wrong?". The duties in Part 4.3 fall on the "law practice" (his firm); for him they mean what
-- his firm may agree and recover, and that a breach is "capable of constituting unsatisfactory professional conduct or
-- professional misconduct" on the part of any associate "involved in the contravention" (Uniform Law ss 181(8), 183(3)).
--
-- Instruments and editions: see lpula-practice.l4. [Application Act] = this subject; [Uniform Law] = the applied law
-- (mrdoc 46913); [General Rules] = Legal Profession Uniform General Rules 2015 (mrdoc 45732). The General Rules specify no
-- legislation for Uniform Law s 181(7)(c) and no rule about uplift fees (searched: no "181", "182" or "uplift" in the deposit).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: disclosure (Uniform Law ss 174-178) beyond Application Act s 143; costs agreements with third party payers
-- (s 180(1)(d)); billing (Division 5) and costs assessment procedure (Division 7) beyond Application Act ss 149, 151; the
-- content of any costs determination (an INPUT); the interest rate under Application Act s 150(3); the Civil Liability Act 2002
-- s 15L and the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 s 27A (the s 141 note); whether the client's arbitration
-- agreement binds (the Commercial Arbitration Act 2012, a separate subject); the review machinery of costs determinations
-- (ss 137-139, 299) other than as one question about when one comes into force.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Legal Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2022 (WA) -- costs`

§§ `Uniform Law Sch 4 cl 18 -- does Part 4.3 govern this matter`

-- [Uniform Law] Sch 4 cl 18(1) (applied by Application Act s 266(2)(a)): "(a) Part 4.3 of this Law applies to a matter if the
-- client first instructs the law practice on or after the commencement day; and (b) the provisions of the old legislation
-- relating to legal costs ... continue to apply to a matter if the client first instructed the law practice in the matter before
-- the commencement day". cl 18(2) (a practice retained by another practice) is not encoded.
@export Does Uniform Law Part 4.3 (legal costs) govern my fees in this matter (Uniform Law Sch 4 cl 18)?
GIVEN `the date the client first instructed us` IS A DATE @desc The date the client first instructed your firm in the matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does Part 4.3 govern my fees in this matter` IF
    `is on or after` `the date the client first instructed us` `commencement day`

§§ `Uniform Law ss 6, 181(7) -- the kind of matter`

-- [Uniform Law] s 181(7): "A conditional costs agreement may relate to any matter, except a matter that involves -- (a) criminal
-- proceedings; or (b) proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975 of the Commonwealth; or (c) proceedings under legislation specified
-- in the Uniform Rules for the purposes of this section." The General Rules specify none.
-- s 6: "litigious matter means a matter that involves, or is likely to involve, the issue of proceedings in a court or tribunal";
-- "A matter is a litigious matter when proceedings are initiated or at any stage when proceedings are reasonably likely."
-- FORK. The Uniform Law defines neither "court" nor "tribunal". Whether an arbitral tribunal is a "tribunal" here is not settled by
-- the text; it is asked as a reading. The Law does use "arbitration proceedings" separately from proceedings "before ... courts"
-- (s 69(2)(b), (c)), which leans against reading it in (INFERENCE). Either way, an arbitration is "likely to involve" court
-- proceedings if a court application (a stay, or enforcing an award) is reasonably likely -- asked as a fact.
DECLARE `My matter` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings`
    `proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975`
    `an arbitration`
    `civil proceedings in a court or tribunal`
    `advice or a transaction with no proceedings`

@export May my firm's costs agreement for this matter be a conditional costs agreement (Uniform Law s 181(7))?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc What kind of matter it is
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the costs agreement be conditional for this matter` IF
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `criminal proceedings` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Is my matter a litigious matter (Uniform Law s 6)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc What kind of matter it is
      `reading an arbitral tribunal as a tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether to read "tribunal" in s 6 as including an arbitral tribunal (the text does not settle it)
      `court or tribunal proceedings are reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings in a court or tribunal are reasonably likely (for an arbitration: a stay or enforcement application)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my matter a litigious matter` IF
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `an arbitration` THEN `reading an arbitral tribunal as a tribunal` OR `court or tribunal proceedings are reasonably likely`
    WHEN `advice or a transaction with no proceedings` THEN `court or tribunal proceedings are reasonably likely`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Uniform Law ss 180-183 -- the agreement`

-- [Uniform Law] s 180(2): "A costs agreement must be written or evidenced in writing." s 181(1): a conditional costs agreement
-- makes payment "conditional on the successful outcome of the matter". s 181(2): it "must -- (a) be in writing and in plain
-- language; and (b) set out the circumstances that constitute the successful outcome". s 181(3): it "must -- (a) be signed by the
-- client; and (b) include a statement that the client has been informed of the client's rights to seek independent legal
-- advice". s 181(4): "a cooling-off period of not less than 5 clear business days ... but this requirement does not apply where
-- the agreement is made between law practices only".
-- s 182(1): it "may provide for the payment of an uplift fee" (s 6: "additional legal costs (excluding disbursements) payable ...
-- on the successful outcome"). s 182(2): for a litigious matter "(a) ... not ... unless the law practice has a reasonable belief
-- that a successful outcome of the matter is reasonably likely; and (b) the uplift fee must not exceed 25% of the legal costs
-- (excluding disbursements) otherwise payable". s 182(3): it "(a) must identify the basis on which the uplift fee is to be
-- calculated; and (b) must include an estimate of the uplift fee or ... a range of estimates ... and an explanation of the major
-- variables".
-- s 183(1): a law practice "must not enter into a costs agreement under which the amount payable ... is calculated by reference to
-- the amount of any award or settlement or the value of any property that may be recovered". s 183(2): not "to the extent that
-- the costs agreement adopts an applicable fixed costs legislative provision". [Application Act] s 136: "a costs determination is
-- a fixed costs legislative provision".
DECLARE `My conditional costs agreement` HAS
    `written and in plain language`                            IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 180(2), s 181(2)(a)
    `it sets out what counts as a successful outcome`          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 181(2)(b)
    `signed by the client`                                     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 181(3)(a)
    `it states the client was told of the right to independent advice` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 181(3)(b)
    `cooling-off period in clear business days`                IS A NUMBER   -- s 181(4)
    `made between law practices only`                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 181(4)
    `it provides for an uplift fee`                            IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 182(1)
    `uplift as a percentage of legal costs excluding disbursements` IS A NUMBER -- s 182(2)(b)
    `my firm reasonably believes success is reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 182(2)(a)
    `it identifies the basis of the uplift and estimates it`   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 182(3)
    `fees calculated by reference to the award, settlement or property recovered` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 183(1)
    `that calculation adopts an applicable costs determination` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 183(2), Application Act s 136

@export Does my firm's conditional costs agreement meet the form requirements of Uniform Law ss 180 and 181?
GIVEN `the agreement` IS A `My conditional costs agreement` @desc The terms of the costs agreement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the agreement meet the form requirements` IF
        `the agreement`'s `written and in plain language`
    AND `the agreement`'s `it sets out what counts as a successful outcome`
    AND `the agreement`'s `signed by the client`
    AND `the agreement`'s `it states the client was told of the right to independent advice`
    AND (   `the agreement`'s `made between law practices only`
         OR `the agreement`'s `cooling-off period in clear business days` AT LEAST 5)

@export Is the uplift fee in my firm's conditional costs agreement allowed (Uniform Law s 182)?
GIVEN `the agreement` IS A `My conditional costs agreement` @desc The terms of the costs agreement
      `a litigious matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is a litigious matter (Uniform Law s 6)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the uplift fee allowed` IF
        (NOT `the agreement`'s `it provides for an uplift fee`)
    OR  (    `the agreement`'s `it identifies the basis of the uplift and estimates it`
         AND (   (NOT `a litigious matter`)
              OR (    `the agreement`'s `my firm reasonably believes success is reasonably likely`
                  AND `the agreement`'s `uplift as a percentage of legal costs excluding disbursements` AT MOST 25)))

@export Is my firm's costs agreement a prohibited contingency fee (Uniform Law s 183, Application Act s 136)?
GIVEN `the agreement` IS A `My conditional costs agreement` @desc The terms of the costs agreement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the agreement a prohibited contingency fee` IF
        `the agreement`'s `fees calculated by reference to the award, settlement or property recovered`
    AND (NOT `the agreement`'s `that calculation adopts an applicable costs determination`)

§§ `Uniform Law ss 184, 185 -- what follows`

-- [Uniform Law] s 184: "Subject to this Law, a costs agreement may be enforced in the same way as any other contract."
-- s 185(1): "A costs agreement that contravenes, or is entered into in contravention of, any provision of this Division is void."
-- s 185(2): the practice may recover no more than "if the costs agreement had not been void". s 185(3): for a contravention of
-- s 182 it "is not entitled to recover the whole or any part of the uplift fee and must repay" it. s 185(4): for a contravention
-- of s 183 it "is not entitled to recover any amount in respect of the provision of legal services in the matter ... and must
-- repay any amount received". Where several defects coexist, the answer names the one with the harshest consequence first
-- (s 183, then s 182, then the rest) -- an ordering choice of this encoding, not of the text.
DECLARE `Agreement answer` IS ONE OF
    `valid: it may be enforced like any other contract (s 184)`
    `void: a contingency fee, so my firm may recover nothing for the services and must repay what it received (s 185(4))`
    `void: the uplift breaches s 182, so no part of the uplift fee is recoverable (s 185(3))`
    `void: this matter cannot have a conditional costs agreement, so no more is recoverable than without it (s 185(1), (2))`
    `void: it fails the form requirements, so no more is recoverable than without it (s 185(1), (2))`

@export Is my firm's conditional costs agreement valid, and if not what can it recover (Uniform Law ss 181-185)?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `My matter` @desc What kind of matter it is
      `reading an arbitral tribunal as a tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether to read "tribunal" in s 6 as including an arbitral tribunal
      `court or tribunal proceedings are reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether court or tribunal proceedings are reasonably likely
      `the agreement` IS A `My conditional costs agreement` @desc The terms of the costs agreement
GIVETH AN `Agreement answer`
DECIDE `Is my conditional costs agreement valid` IS
    IF `Is the agreement a prohibited contingency fee` `the agreement`
    THEN `void: a contingency fee, so my firm may recover nothing for the services and must repay what it received (s 185(4))`
    ELSE IF NOT `Is the uplift fee allowed` `the agreement`
                    (`Is my matter a litigious matter` `the matter` `reading an arbitral tribunal as a tribunal` `court or tribunal proceedings are reasonably likely`)
         THEN `void: the uplift breaches s 182, so no part of the uplift fee is recoverable (s 185(3))`
         ELSE IF NOT `May the costs agreement be conditional for this matter` `the matter`
              THEN `void: this matter cannot have a conditional costs agreement, so no more is recoverable than without it (s 185(1), (2))`
              ELSE IF NOT `Does the agreement meet the form requirements` `the agreement`
                   THEN `void: it fails the form requirements, so no more is recoverable than without it (s 185(1), (2))`
                   ELSE `valid: it may be enforced like any other contract (s 184)`

-- [Uniform Law] s 182(4) and s 183(1): "Civil penalty: 100 penalty units" -- on the law practice. For the lawyer: s 181(8) and
-- s 183(3) make a contravention "capable of constituting unsatisfactory professional conduct or professional misconduct on the
-- part of any principal of the law practice or any legal practitioner associate ... involved in the contravention".
@export What is the most my firm can be ordered to pay for an uplift or contingency-fee breach (Uniform Law ss 182(4), 183(1)), in penalty units?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most my firm can be ordered to pay` IS 100

@export Can a breach of the conditional costs or contingency fee rules be a conduct matter against me (Uniform Law ss 181(8), 183(3))?
GIVEN `my firm contravened ss 181-183` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your firm contravened the rules on conditional costs agreements, uplift fees or contingency fees
      `I was involved in the contravention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a principal or associate involved in it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the breach be a conduct matter against me` IF
    `my firm contravened ss 181-183` AND `I was involved in the contravention`

§§ `Application Act ss 3, 132-134 -- may a WA costs determination cover my work`

-- [Application Act] s 3: "contentious business means legal services by an Australian legal practitioner in or for the purposes of
-- any action, suit or proceedings before a court, but does not include the administration of estates and trusts";
-- "noncontentious business means legal services other than contentious business".
-- s 132: "In this Division -- court includes an arbitrator, a tribunal or a person, with the power to require the production of
-- documents or the answering of questions."
-- s 133(1): the Legal Costs Committee "may make legal costs determinations regulating the costs that may be charged by law
-- practices in respect of -- (a) noncontentious business; and (b) contentious business before -- (i) the Supreme Court; or (ii)
-- the District Court; or (iii) the Magistrates Court; or (iv) a court of summary jurisdiction; or (v) the State Administrative
-- Tribunal; or (vi) the Family Court of Western Australia; or (vii) any other court declared by the Attorney General under section
-- 134(1)".
-- FORK (carried from the first-pass encoding). If "court" in the s 3 definition keeps its ordinary meaning, arbitration work is
-- noncontentious business and s 133(1)(a) reaches it. If s 132 is read into it, the arbitration is contentious business before a
-- "court" that s 133(1)(b) does not list, reached only by a s 134(1) declaration. Both readings are asserted.
DECLARE Forum IS ONE OF
    `no proceedings`
    `the Supreme Court`
    `the District Court`
    `the Magistrates Court`
    `the State Administrative Tribunal`
    `the Family Court of Western Australia`
    `an arbitrator`
    `the Federal Court of Australia`

GIVEN `the forum` IS A Forum
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `listed in s 133(1)(b)` IF
    CONSIDER `the forum`
    WHEN `the Supreme Court` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the District Court` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Magistrates Court` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the State Administrative Tribunal` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Family Court of Western Australia` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export May a WA legal costs determination cover my work (Application Act ss 3, 132-134)?
GIVEN `the forum` IS A Forum @desc Where the work is done
      `administration of estates or trusts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work is the administration of estates and trusts
      `reading s 132 into contentious business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether to read s 132's wider "court" (including an arbitrator) into the s 3 definition (the text does not settle it)
      `declared under s 134(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Attorney General has declared the forum under s 134(1)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a costs determination cover my work` IF
    IF `contentious`
    THEN `listed in s 133(1)(b)` `the forum` OR `declared under s 134(1)`
    ELSE TRUE
    WHERE
        `contentious` MEANS
                (NOT `administration of estates or trusts`)
            AND CONSIDER `the forum`
                WHEN `no proceedings` THEN FALSE
                WHEN `an arbitrator` THEN `reading s 132 into contentious business`
                OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Application Act ss 140, 141, 143 -- when a determination governs my fees`

-- [Application Act] s 140(3): a determination "comes into force on -- (a) the day on which the report of the determination is
-- published ...; or (b) if a later day is specified in the report -- that day".
@export From what date does a WA costs determination govern my fees (Application Act s 140(3))?
GIVEN `the date the report was published` IS A DATE @desc The date the Legal Costs Committee's report was published
      `a later day is specified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report specifies a later day
      `the specified day` IS A DATE @desc The day specified (any date if none)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `From when does the determination govern my fees` IS
    IF `a later day is specified` AND Day `the specified day` GREATER THAN Day `the date the report was published`
    THEN `the specified day`
    ELSE `the date the report was published`

-- s 141(1): "The following are regulated by an applicable costs determination -- (a) the taxation of bills of law practices; (b)
-- any other aspect of the costs charged by law practices." s 141(2): "subject to -- (a) any costs agreement made in accordance with
-- the Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) Part 4.3 Division 4 ...; and (b) section 146; and (c) the Legal Aid Commission Act 1976
-- section 14". s 141(3): a court finding the amount "inadequate because of the unusual difficulty, complexity or importance of the
-- matter" may order more. So a valid conditional costs agreement governs instead of the determination; a void one does not.
DECLARE `My costs position` HAS
    `an applicable costs determination exists`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `a costs agreement made in accordance with Part 4.3 Division 4` IS A BOOLEAN
    `Legal Aid Commission Act s 14 or Application Act s 146 applies` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a court found the determination inadequate for unusual difficulty, complexity or importance` IS A BOOLEAN

@export Does a WA costs determination govern what my firm may charge (Application Act s 141)?
GIVEN `the position` IS A `My costs position` @desc The determination, agreement and orders in the matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does a costs determination govern my fees` IF
        `the position`'s `an applicable costs determination exists`
    AND (NOT `the position`'s `a costs agreement made in accordance with Part 4.3 Division 4`)
    AND (NOT `the position`'s `Legal Aid Commission Act s 14 or Application Act s 146 applies`)
    AND (NOT `the position`'s `a court found the determination inadequate for unusual difficulty, complexity or importance`)

-- s 143: "When a law practice provides a client with information under the Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) section 174(1)(a), the
-- law practice must provide the client with information about whether the legal costs are subject to a costs determination."
@export Has my firm breached Application Act s 143 by not saying whether a costs determination applies?
GIVEN `we gave the Uniform Law s 174(1)(a) disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your firm gave the client the s 174(1)(a) costs disclosure
      `we said whether a costs determination applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it also told the client whether a costs determination applies
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my firm breached section 143` IF
    `we gave the Uniform Law s 174(1)(a) disclosure` AND (NOT `we said whether a costs determination applies`)

§§ `Application Act ss 148, 149, 151 -- after a costs assessment`

-- s 149(2): an application for review by the Supreme Court "must be made -- (a) within 28 days after the day on which the party is
-- notified of the costs assessment". s 148: otherwise the assessment "is binding on all parties".
@export Can I still apply to the Supreme Court for review of the costs assessment (Application Act s 149(2)(a))?
GIVEN `the date I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The date you (or your firm) were notified of the costs assessment
      `the date I apply` IS A DATE @desc The date of the review application
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I still apply for review of the assessment` IF
        Day `the date I apply` AT LEAST Day `the date I was notified`
    AND Day `the date I apply` MINUS Day `the date I was notified` AT MOST 28

-- s 151: "If a person paid an amount as legal costs and that amount exceeds the legal costs determined to be payable on a costs
-- assessment ... the excess amount is recoverable in a court of competent jurisdiction as a debt due to the person."
@export How much must my firm repay as excess over the assessed costs (Application Act s 151)?
GIVEN `paid` IS A NUMBER @desc What the client paid as legal costs, in dollars
      `assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs determined on assessment, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much must my firm repay` IS
    IF `paid` GREATER THAN `assessed` THEN `paid` MINUS `assessed` ELSE 0
